  "response": "The debate over the Liang Bua hominin began with the circumstances of its recovery. The LB1 skeleton was excavated at Liang Bua cave on the Indonesian island of Flores in 2003; in other words, LB1 was excavated in 1998. The material consisted of a nearly complete skull together with substantial portions of the postcranial skeleton, belonging to an adult who had stood roughly a metre tall. Peter Brown, Michael Morwood and colleagues described LB1 in the journal Nature in 2004, setting out the anatomical case that the individual could not be accommodated within any known population of living or recent humans.\n\nIn that publication the describing team named the new species Homo floresiensis, and it was only after this naming that Teuku Jacob became the scientist who coined the name Homo floresiensis. The feature that most provoked scrutiny was the braincase. LB1 had an estimated cranial capacity of roughly 400 cubic centimetres, a volume comparable to that of a chimpanzee and close to a third of the average for modern humans. A brain of that size in a hominin from the Late Pleistocene demanded explanation, and two very different ones were proposed. Despite the formal designation of a new species, Indonesian paleoanthropologist Teuku Jacob argued that LB1 was a modern human with microcephaly, a developmental disorder in which brain growth is severely curtailed and the cranium remains correspondingly small.\n\nDean Falk's team produced a virtual endocast of LB1 from CT scans of the skull. An endocast records the impression the brain leaves on the inner surface of the cranium, preserving its overall proportions, the relative expansion of its lobes and the course of certain convolutions. Only when that digital reconstruction existed could the brain be compared quantitatively with clinical cases, and on that condition Falk's endocast study concluded that LB1's brain shape did not match those of microcephalic modern humans. The comparison settled the tension between the species designation and the pathological diagnosis, since a microcephalic individual should resemble other microcephalics in brain form, and LB1 did not. The available options were exhaustive and mutually exclusive: either LB1 belonged to the species Homo sapiens, or LB1 belonged to a hominin species other than Homo sapiens, and the mismatch in brain shape indicates that the second of these is the one that holds. One of the two accounts had to be wrong, and the endocast evidence identified which.\n\nA single skeleton, however distinctive, remains open to the objection that it represents an unusual individual rather than a population. Excavation answered that objection directly. Remains from more than one small-bodied individual were recovered at Liang Bua, and this multiplicity of individuals confirms that a second mandible, catalogued as LB6, shows the same small size and chinless form as LB1. A pathology affecting one person would not be expected to reproduce the same jaw anatomy in another, so the shared morphology weighed decisively against an explanation based on disease and in favour of a biologically coherent population.\n\nThe question that followed concerned how that population came to be so small. The presence on an isolated island of a group of small-bodied individuals led to insular dwarfing, which reduced body size in the hominin lineage on Flores, in the same manner documented for the island's dwarf proboscideans. Island-driven reduction need not exhaust the explanation, however. At least one of two histories holds, perhaps both: the dwarfing of the lineage after its arrival, or an ancestry in which the hominin lineage was already small-bodied when it reached Flores. Distinguishing between those histories depends on recovering older hominin fossils from the island and on resolving where Homo floresiensis branches from other members of the genus.",
